  "answer_core": "Slow the situation down and focus on keeping your child safe without turning this into a panic reaction that pushes the contact underground. Stay calm with your daughter and keep the door open for her to talk. Let her know you’re concerned because of the age gap and that you want to understand what’s happening, not punish her.\n\nBefore deciding whether to report, talk with a therapist who works with adolescents, RAINN, or legal counsel. They can help you gauge risk and next steps based on what you’re seeing.\n\nKeep your co‑parent in the loop enough to stay aligned on safety, but try to keep the conversation out of a blame frame.\n\nAvoid suddenly shutting her phone down unless a clear safety tripwire fires. Those tripwires include: the older person asking to meet in person; requests for or sharing of sexual images; any threats; evidence they are contacting other minors; your child saying she feels pressured or scared; or the person moving to a hidden channel after you’ve set boundaries. If any of these happen, act quickly: secure the device, preserve messages, and get professional help for next steps.\n\nEven if her phone looks quiet, treat that as weak evidence that the contact has stopped. It may just have shifted to a different app or account.\n\nKeep checking in with your daughter, reassure her she’s not in trouble, and let professionals help guide your decisions so you can protect her while preserving trust.",
